{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-93735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-93735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving the second equation for ${x}_{2}$ and substituting it into the first equation, we obtain the following expressions for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})^{2}+3({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})(-{y}_{2})+(-{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}-3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-93735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "ca