500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving these for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(-{y}_{2})^{2}+(-{y}_{2}){y}_{1}-2{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}-2{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8fe894d9291c4fc4f32584c7c7203449ff475c40516ebf57451b51075bbdad6"} {"question" "q-94180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "-{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8fe894d9291c4fc4f32584c7c7203449ff475c40516ebf57451b51075bbdad6"} {"question" "q-94182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}10 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 12\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 15 \\\\ 15 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 32\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 25 \\\\ 25 & 6\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 32\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 13 \\\\ 3 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}47 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 19\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8fe894d9291c4fc4f32584c7c7203449ff475c40516ebf57451b51075bbdad6"} {"question" "q-94183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 11\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}27 & 15 \\\\ 15 & 10\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}27 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}47 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 19\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}47 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 19\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185bc05f776e7691ea9d74213de5a9"} {"question" "q-94187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072500/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5699c2e702181b3e038127dffeb79a682185b